# dash/safe.bd - trace and IDS verdicts.
#
# IT REPORTS. IT NEVER ACTS. This is the whole design rule.
#
# The old shell/ suite's check_trace() called disconnect()/exit()/raise()
# inline. That is fine in a one-shot script and fatal in a long-running
# panel: exit() would kill the dashboard from inside a helper, and raise()
# would unwind the event loop past everything holding the UI together. So
# every function here returns a verdict string and the loop decides what to
# do about it.
#
# All reads are 0 ticks (get_trace_time, ids_status). The only cost is the
# lines themselves, which is why this is one function and not five.
#
# VERDICTS, worst first:
#   abort     IDS is actively watching - get off the box now
#   hopeless  trace ceiling is too low to do anything useful with
#   cool      trace is running out; disconnect and let it decay
#   flagged   noticed but not yet urgent
#   ok        proceed

# Both are SECONDS REMAINING on the live trace, not the box's trace ceiling.
# Tuned 2026-09-16 for a test server with a 20s ceiling: at the old COOL=30
# every session on that box would have been "cool" from the instant it
# opened, auto-dropping before anything could be observed.
#
# COOL must stay above HOPELESS or it becomes dead code - check() tests
# hopeless first, so equal values mean "cool" can never be reached.
#
# NOT HANDLED YET: the plan's other sense of "hopeless" - a box whose trace
# CEILING is so low it is not worth attacking at all, judged from
# connect()'s trace_time before starting work. That is a different test from
# this one and wants its own gate.
var HOPELESS = 5
var COOL = 10

# `ceiling` is connect()'s own trace_time - this box's MAXIMUM, captured once
# when the session opened, as opposed to `t` which is what is left right now.
#
# A ceiling of 0 means the box does not trace at all. Without this the
# remaining-time tests read 0 as the worst possible value and would auto-drop
# an untraced box instantly - exactly backwards. Every trace test is
# therefore gated on ceiling > 0, and "none" is reported so the state is
# visible rather than silently indistinguishable from "ok".
#
# HONEST LIMIT: we cannot tell "this box never traces" from "the trace has
# already completed" - both look like 0 from here. Reporting "none" instead
# of "safe" leaves that judgement with the player. IDS is checked
# independently and still aborts on an untraced box, since the two systems
# are separate.
func check(conn, ceiling):
    var t = get_trace_time()
    var i = ids_status(conn)
    var v = "ok"
    if i == "active":
        v = "abort"
    elif ceiling > 0 and t <= HOPELESS:
        v = "hopeless"
    elif ceiling > 0 and t <= COOL:
        v = "cool"
    elif i == "flagged":
        v = "flagged"
    elif ceiling <= 0:
        v = "none"
    return {"trace": t, "ids": i, "verdict": v}
